"""
Administrative Law Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: Delegated legislation, natural justice, ultra vires, judicial review, tribunals, administrative discretion, writs.
"""

admin_law_easy = [
    ("What is the primary branch of public law that governs the activities and powers of administrative agencies?", "Administrative Law", ["Private International Law", "Admiralty Law", "Patent Law"], "Administrative law deals with the powers, procedures, and judicial control of administrative authorities."),
    ("The core legal maxim 'Nemo judex in causa sua' fundamentally means:", "No one should be a judge in their own cause (Rule against bias)", ["Hear the other side before deciding", "Let the buyer beware", "The law does not concern itself with trifles"], "Nemo judex in causa sua ensures impartial adjudication by disqualifying judges with personal or pecuniary bias."),
    ("The core legal maxim 'Audi alteram partem' fundamentally means:", "Hear the other side (Rule of fair hearing)", ["Ignorance of law is no excuse", "An act of god injures no one", "The king can do no wrong"], "Audi alteram partem requires giving reasonable notice and an opportunity to be heard before an adverse decision is taken."),
    ("Law made by an executive authority under statutory powers delegated by Parliament is known as:", "Delegated Legislation (Subordinate Legislation)", ["Primary Legislation", "Ordinance", "Judicial Precedent"], "Delegated legislation includes statutory rules, regulations, orders, notifications, and bye-laws."),
    ("Which doctrine invalidates administrative rules that exceed the powers granted by the parent Act?", "The Doctrine of Ultra Vires", ["The Doctrine of Severability", "The Doctrine of Eclipse", "The Doctrine of Pith and Substance"], "Ultra vires ('beyond powers') renders any subordinate rule void if it conflicts with or exceeds the parent statute."),
    ("Under which Article of the Constitution can an aggrieved citizen approach the Supreme Court directly for writ remedies?", "Article 32", ["Article 226", "Article 136", "Article 143"], "Article 32 guarantees the fundamental right to move the Supreme Court for enforcement of Part III rights."),
    ("Under which Article of the Constitution can High Courts issue writs for fundamental and other legal rights?", "Article 226", ["Article 32", "Article 131", "Article 142"], "Article 226 grants High Courts wide discretionary jurisdiction to issue writs for fundamental and statutory rights."),
    ("Which prerogative writ is issued to release a person unlawfully or illegally detained in custody?", "Habeas Corpus", ["Mandamus", "Certiorari", "Quo-Warranto"], "Habeas Corpus ('you shall have the body') directs the custodian to produce the detainee and justify detention."),
    ("Which writ is issued by superior courts to command a public authority to perform a mandatory statutory duty?", "Mandamus", ["Prohibition", "Certiorari", "Habeas Corpus"], "Mandamus ('we command') compels public officials to discharge mandatory non-discretionary duties."),
    ("Which writ is issued by a superior court to prevent an inferior tribunal from exceeding its jurisdiction?", "Prohibition", ["Mandamus", "Habeas Corpus", "Quo-Warranto"], "Prohibition is a preventive writ issued while proceedings are pending to stop unauthorized jurisdiction."),
    ("Which writ is issued by a superior court to quash a final order passed by an inferior court without jurisdiction?", "Certiorari", ["Mandamus", "Prohibition", "Habeas Corpus"], "Certiorari is a curative writ that calls up lower court records to quash orders tainted by jurisdictional defects."),
    ("Which writ inquires into the lawful title or warrant by which a person holds a public substantive office?", "Quo-Warranto", ["Mandamus", "Certiorari", "Prohibition"], "Quo-Warranto ('by what authority') ousts illegal usurpers from public statutory offices."),
    ("An administrative decision rendered in violation of the Principles of Natural Justice is:", "Void and a nullity in law", ["Fully valid and binding on all parties", "A minor technical irregularity that cannot be challenged", "Immune from constitutional judicial review"], "Natural justice violations deprive the authority of jurisdiction, rendering the resulting order void ab initio."),
    ("The Central Administrative Tribunal (CAT) in India was established in 1985 pursuant to which Article?", "Article 323A", ["Article 323B", "Article 262", "Article 280"], "Article 323A empowers Parliament to establish administrative tribunals for civil service recruitment and conditions."),
    ("The Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013 was enacted to establish an anti-corruption ombudsman at which levels?", "Lokpal at the Centre and Lokayuktas at the State level", ["Lokpal at the Gram Panchayat level exclusively", "Lokpal at international embassies only", "Lokayuktas exclusively in the Supreme Court"], "The Act establishes an independent statutory ombudsman to inquire into allegations of corruption against public officials."),
    ("Under Article 299 of the Constitution, contracts made by the Union of India must be expressed to be made by:", "The President of India", ["The Prime Minister of India", "The Chief Justice of India", "The Comptroller and Auditor General"], "Article 299 requires government contracts to be executed on behalf of the President or Governor by authorized officers."),
    ("Which parliamentary committee scrutinizes statutory rules and orders framed under delegated legislation?", "Committee on Subordinate Legislation", ["Public Accounts Committee", "Estimates Committee", "Business Advisory Committee"], "The Committee on Subordinate Legislation in both Houses ensures delegated powers are not abused or exceeded."),
    ("The rule that a judge or adjudicator with a direct financial stake in the outcome is disqualified is called:", "Pecuniary Bias", ["Personal Bias", "Subject-Matter Bias", "Policy Bias"], "Any direct pecuniary interest, however small, automatically disqualifies an adjudicator without needing proof of actual bias."),
    ("In administrative law, a 'Speaking Order' is an order that:", "Contains clear, cogent reasons justifying the decision reached", ["Is delivered orally in a public auditorium", "Is broadcast on national public radio", "Is signed exclusively by a sitting judge"], "Speaking orders ensure transparency, prevent arbitrariness, and enable effective judicial review of reasons."),
    ("Public Interest Litigation (PIL) relaxed which traditional procedural requirement in constitutional courts?", "Locus Standi (the strict requirement of being personally aggrieved)", ["The requirement of having a written constitution", "The existence of the Supreme Court bench", "The payment of judicial salaries"], "PIL allows any public-spirited citizen or NGO to move the court to enforce rights of disadvantaged groups."),
    ("Under the RTI Act 2005, who is designated in every public authority to receive requests for information?", "Central / State Public Information Officer (CPIO / SPIO)", ["Chief Vigilance Officer", "District Magistrate", "Registrar General"], "Section 5 of the RTI Act mandates appointment of PIOs to process information requests within statutory timelines."),
    ("What is the maximum penalty that can be imposed on a PIO under Section 20 of the RTI Act for willful default?", "Rs 25,000 (at Rs 250 per day of delay)", ["Rs 1,00,000", "Rs 5,000", "Rs 50,000"], "The Information Commission can impose fines up to Rs 25,000 on defaulting PIOs for malafide delay or denial."),
    ("Which constitutional principle guarantees that state action must not be arbitrary, whimsical, or discriminatory?", "Article 14 (Equality before Law / Non-arbitrariness)", ["Article 25", "Article 31", "Article 51"], "E.P. Royappa established that non-arbitrariness is the core essence of Article 14 equality."),
    ("The doctrine that the Crown / State cannot be sued in tort without consent is known historically as:", "Sovereign Immunity", ["Strict Liability", "Absolute Immunity", "Res Judicata"], "Historically derived from 'the King can do no wrong', sovereign immunity has been progressively restricted in India."),
    ("When an administrative authority exercises power for an unauthorized, improper, or corrupt purpose, it commits:", "Mala fides (Bad Faith) / Malice in law", ["Bona fide error", "Subordinate delegation", "Judicial precedent"], "Malice in law invalidates administrative action taken for purposes other than those authorized by statute."),
    ("The doctrine that an administrative authority cannot fetter its future discretion by rigid pre-determined rules is:", "The Rule against Fettering Discretion", ["The Doctrine of Severability", "The Rule of Lenity", "The Doctrine of Estoppel"], "Authorities must genuinely consider the merits of each individual application rather than rigidly applying blanket bans."),
    ("When an administrative rule conflicts with the Parent Act under which it was framed, which prevails?", "The Parent Act prevails, and the conflicting rule is void", ["The subordinate rule automatically amends the Parent Act", "Both apply concurrently through compromise", "The subordinate rule prevails as it is more recent"], "Subordinate legislation is inferior to primary parliamentary statutes and cannot override parent enactments."),
    ("The concept of 'Post-Decisional Hearing' in administrative natural justice was recognized in which landmark case?", "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)", ["A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras (1950)", "Shankari Prasad v. Union of India (1951)", "Golaknath v. State of Punjab (1967)"], "Where prior notice is impossible due to urgency, a fair post-decisional hearing satisfies natural justice."),
    ("In administrative law, the rule that an authority cannot sub-delegate delegated legislative power is expressed as:", "'Delegatus non potest delegare'", ["'Caveat emptor'", "'Res ipsa loquitur'", "'Stare decisis'"], "A delegate cannot further delegate discretionary legislative powers unless expressly authorized by the parent Act."),
    ("The legal mechanism where citizens file writ petitions via simple letters or postcards is known as:", "Epistolary Jurisdiction of the Supreme Court", ["Appellate Civil Jurisdiction", "Advisory Reference Jurisdiction", "Contempt Jurisdiction"], "Chief Justices P.N. Bhagwati and V.R. Krishna Iyer treated letters and post-cards from prisoners as PIL writ petitions."),
    ("Which writ cannot be issued against a purely private entity or private company lacking public duty?", "Mandamus", ["Habeas Corpus", "Quo-Warranto", "Injunction"], "Mandamus lies only against public authorities, statutory corporations, or entities discharging public statutory functions."),
    ("The Central Information Commission (CIC) is constituted by the Central Government under which chapter of the RTI Act?", "Chapter III (Section 12) of the RTI Act, 2005", ["Chapter I", "Chapter VI", "Schedule II"], "Section 12 provides for the constitution of the Central Information Commission headed by Chief Information Commissioner."),
    ("Which section of the Commissions of Inquiry Act, 1952 empowers government to appoint an inquiry commission?", "Section 3", ["Section 12", "Section 21", "Section 35"], "Section 3 empowers the appropriate government to appoint a Commission of Inquiry on a matter of public importance."),
    ("The official appointed under the Lokpal Act to head the independent investigation wing of Lokpal is the:", "Director of Inquiry / Director of Prosecution", ["Central Vigilance Commissioner", "Attorney General", "Solicitor General"], "Lokpal possesses an Inquiry Wing and a Prosecution Wing to investigate and prosecute corrupt public officials.")
]

admin_law_med = [
    ("In constitutional administrative law, what is the core test to distinguish 'Conditional Legislation' from 'Delegated Legislation'?", "Conditional legislation leaves the timing/area of application to executive; Delegated legislation permits rule-making", ["Delegated legislation is passed by courts; Conditional legislation by ministers", "Conditional legislation requires a public referendum; Delegated legislation does not", "There is no constitutional difference between the two concepts"], "In conditional legislation, the legislature determines policy completely and delegates only the contingency of enforcement."),
    ("Which landmark case established that Parliament cannot delegate 'Essential Legislative Functions' to the executive?", "In re Delhi Laws Act Case (1951)", ["A.K. Kraipak v. Union of India (1969)", "Kesavananda Bharati Case (1973)", "Minerva Mills Case (1980)"], "The Supreme Court held that laying down legislative policy and standard is non-delegable essential function."),
    ("What are the two distinct judicial heads under which delegated legislation can be struck down as Ultra Vires?", "Substantive Ultra Vires (exceeding subject powers) and Procedural Ultra Vires (violating mandatory procedure)", ["Civil Ultra Vires and Criminal Ultra Vires", "Constitutional Ultra Vires and International Ultra Vires", "Temporal Ultra Vires and Spatial Ultra Vires"], "Substantive ultra vires challenges content beyond parent Act; procedural ultra vires challenges failure to follow statutory processes."),
    ("In A.K. Kraipak v. Union of India (1969), the Supreme Court decisively broke down the artificial distinction between:", "Quasi-judicial administrative functions and purely administrative functions for natural justice", ["Criminal proceedings and civil lawsuits", "Primary parliamentary statutes and ordinances", "High Court writs and Supreme Court appeals"], "The Court held that principles of natural justice apply to administrative inquiries whenever rights are affected."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Necessity' in administrative law operates as an emergency exception to which principle?", "The Rule against Bias (Nemo judex in causa sua)", ["The Rule against Sub-delegation", "The Doctrine of Ultra Vires", "The Doctrine of Promissory Estoppel"], "If no other qualified person can adjudicate due to statutory monopoly, a disqualified officer must act by necessity."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Promissory Estoppel' protects a private citizen against the government when:", "The citizen altered their position relying upon a clear, unambiguous executive government representation", ["The citizen bribed a public officer to obtain a commercial license", "The government changes taxation rates through parliamentary acts", "A public official breaks a personal informal promise"], "Government is bound by its representations if a citizen acted upon them to their detriment, unless overriding public interest exists."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Legitimate Expectation' provides procedural and substantive protection to a citizen when:", "Past consistent administrative practice or express promise creates a reasonable expectation of a hearing or outcome", ["A citizen speculates on stock market share price movements", "An applicant hopes to win a government lottery draw", "A private employee expects a salary raise from a private firm"], "Legitimate expectation requires authorities to give a fair hearing before departing from established past practice."),
    ("In the historic ruling L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997), the 7-judge Constitution Bench held that:", "Judicial review of High Courts under Articles 226/227 over Administrative Tribunals is an un-amendable Basic Feature", ["Administrative Tribunals can completely replace High Courts permanently", "Decisions of CAT cannot be challenged in any court", "Articles 323A and 323B are completely unconstitutional"], "All decisions of administrative tribunals must be subject to scrutiny before a Division Bench of the High Court."),
    ("The 'Henry VIII Clause' in delegated legislation is a controversial statutory provision that empowers the executive to:", "Modify or amend the provisions of the primary Act itself by subordinate order to remove difficulties", ["Execute prisoners without any criminal judicial trial", "Dissolve Parliament by executive fiat", "Levy income taxes without parliamentary legislation"], "Henry VIII clauses are scrutinized strictly by courts and permitted only for removing transient implementation difficulties."),
    ("What is the fundamental difference between the legal effects of an administrative order that is 'Void' versus 'Voidable'?", "A void order is non-existent in law from inception; a voidable order remains valid until set aside by a court", ["A void order applies only to foreign citizens", "A voidable order can never be challenged in court", "There is no legal difference between void and voidable orders"], "In administrative jurisprudence, an order passed without jurisdiction or violating natural justice is deemed void."),
    ("The landmark 'Wednesbury Unreasonableness' test for judicial review of administrative discretion originates from:", "Associated Provincial Picture Houses v. Wednesbury Corporation (1948) in the English Court of Appeal", ["Marbury v. Madison (1803) in US Supreme Court", "Donoghue v. Stevenson (1932) in House of Lords", "Hadley v. Baxendale (1854) in Exchequer Court"], "Lord Greene held courts intervene only if an executive decision is so absurd that no sensible person could make it."),
    ("The modern European and Indian public law doctrine of 'Proportionality' differs from Wednesbury review because:", "Proportionality allows courts to examine whether the least intrusive means were used to achieve the objective", ["Proportionality requires courts to rubber-stamp all government choices", "Proportionality applies only to international maritime trade disputes", "Proportionality completely eliminates judicial review"], "Proportionality applies a four-part structured scrutiny: legitimate aim, suitability, necessity, and balancing."),
    ("In S.P. Gupta v. Union of India (1981) (First Judges Case), Justice P.N. Bhagwati established that in PILs:", "Any member of the public acting bona fide can invoke writ jurisdiction for persons unable to reach court", ["Only lawyers with 20 years of practice can file writ petitions", "PILs can be filed only against private corporate cartels", "The petitioner must pay full legal costs of the state"], "The Court broadened locus standi to ensure judicial access for impoverished, illiterate, and marginalized citizens."),
    ("In Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993), the Supreme Court affirmed that constitutional public law damages for custodial death:", "Are a distinct strict liability constitutional remedy independent of private tort claims", ["Require filing a civil lawsuit in a subordinate district court over 20 years", "Cannot be awarded under Article 32 or Article 226", "Are payable only if criminal trial ends in conviction"], "Monetary compensation under Article 32/226 acts as corrective public law remedy for fundamental rights violations."),
    ("In Kasturi Lal v. State of UP (1965), the Supreme Court controversially upheld sovereign immunity because:", "The police seized gold during the exercise of a sovereign state function (arrest and custody)", ["The police were private contractors without government identity", "The plaintiff was an alien enemy without legal rights", "Gold was not recognized as private property"], "Kasturi Lal distinguished sovereign from non-sovereign functions, a distinction extensively eroded in subsequent cases."),
    ("In Rudul Sah v. State of Bihar (1983), the Supreme Court created constitutional history by awarding:", "Monetary compensation under Article 32 for illegal detention of 14 years after acquittal", ["A life imprisonment sentence to the jail warden", "An order declaring all state prisons unconstitutional", "A directive dissolving the state legislative assembly"], "Chief Justice Y.V. Chandrachud ordered interim compensation for gross violation of Article 21 personal liberty."),
    ("In administrative natural justice, the 'Real Likelihood of Bias' test evaluates whether:", "A reasonable, fair-minded observer informed of facts would conclude there was a real possibility of bias", ["The adjudicator signed a written confession admitting corruption", "The adjudicator belongs to the same political party", "The plaintiff disliked the adjudicator's personality"], "The test focuses on reasonable apprehension in the mind of the public, maintaining faith in judicial administration."),
    ("The concept of 'Subject-Matter Bias' in administrative adjudication arises when the decision-maker:", "Has a pre-conceived rigid institutional policy or personal crusade directly involving the dispute issue", ["Owns commercial equity stock in an unrelated company", "Is personally related by marriage to one of the advocates", "Refuses to grant an adjournment to the plaintiff"], "A clear preconceived commitment to a particular outcome deprives the adjudicator of an open judicial mind."),
    ("The rule that 'Justice must not only be done, but must manifestly and undoubtedly be seen to be done' originates from:", "R v. Sussex Justices, ex parte McCarthy (1924) by Lord Hewart", ["Ridge v. Baldwin (1964) by Lord Reid", "Anisminic v. Foreign Compensation Commission (1969)", "Breen v. Amalgamated Engineering Union (1971)"], "Lord Hewart underscored that public confidence in judicial administration demands scrupulous appearance of impartiality."),
    ("In Ridge v. Baldwin (1964), the House of Lords landmark decision revived administrative law by ruling that:", "Natural justice fair hearing applies to administrative dismissals regardless of quasi-judicial classification", ["Police chiefs can be dismissed summarily without any hearing", "Administrative tribunals are completely exempt from natural justice", "Judicial review cannot examine police appointments"], "Lord Reid dismantled the formalistic distinction between administrative and judicial acts concerning natural justice."),
    ("The 'Ouster Clause' (Privative Clause) in a parent statute attempting to exclude judicial review is treated by Indian courts as:", "Ineffective to exclude the constitutional writ review jurisdiction of High Courts and Supreme Court", ["Completely binding, barring all courts from hearing challenges", "Valid only during commercial economic transactions", "Applicable only to private commercial contracts"], "Constitutional writ jurisdiction under Articles 32 and 226 cannot be ousted by ordinary legislative statutes."),
    ("In Anisminic Ltd v. Foreign Compensation Commission (1969), the House of Lords expanded jurisdictional review by establishing that:", "Any error of law made by a tribunal going to jurisdiction renders its decision an ultra vires nullity", ["Tribunals have absolute immunity from judicial errors", "Ouster clauses completely shield tribunals from judicial review", "Only civil courts can commit jurisdictional errors"], "Anisminic abolished the narrow distinction between errors going to jurisdiction and errors within jurisdiction."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Exhaustion of Alternative Remedies' in writ jurisdiction under Article 226 implies that:", "High Courts will ordinarily decline writ relief if an efficacious statutory appeal mechanism is available", ["Aggrieved citizens can never approach High Courts under any circumstance", "Writ petitions must be filed in all 25 High Courts simultaneously", "Citizens must exhaust all personal private financial savings first"], "Exhaustion of remedies is a rule of judicial discretion and policy, not an absolute jurisdictional bar."),
    ("In administrative discretion, 'Non-application of Mind' by an administrative authority is demonstrated when:", "The authority passes an order mechanically without considering facts, or blindly rubber-stamps external directives", ["The authority consults legal advice before writing an order", "The authority takes three months to write an order", "The authority grants a full personal hearing to all parties"], "Acting under dictation from external superiors or using stock proforma templates shows non-application of mind."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Collateral Fact' (Jurisdictional Fact) in administrative law establishes that:", "A court can examine whether preliminary facts necessary to trigger agency jurisdiction actually existed", ["Courts must accept all facts stated by executive officers without review", "Administrative agencies can invent facts to expand jurisdiction", "Jurisdiction cannot depend on any factual preconditions"], "If an authority erroneously determines a jurisdictional fact, its entire resulting proceeding is without jurisdiction."),
    ("In Central Board of Boarding and Professional Education v. Union of India, promissory estoppel was held inapplicable against:", "The exercise of statutory legislative functions or statutes prohibiting the act", ["A private citizen building a residential house", "A commercial bank granting educational loans", "A public corporation entering commercial contracts"], "No promissory estoppel can be invoked against the legislature or to compel government to act against statute."),
    ("The Supreme Court ruled in Common Cause v. Union of India (1999) (Petrol Pump Allocation Case) that:", "Public power is a public trust, and discretionary allotments made on arbitrary personal patronage are illegal", ["Cabinet ministers possess unlimited personal discretion to award licenses to relatives", "Administrative discretion cannot be reviewed by courts under Article 32", "Public fuel dealerships are exempt from public auctions"], "The Court quashed arbitrary fuel pump allotments made by the minister, holding discretionary power must be reasonable."),
    ("In Tata Cellular v. Union of India (1994), the Supreme Court laid down principles for judicial review of government tenders:", "Judicial review examines the decision-making process, not the commercial wisdom of the decision itself", ["Courts should act as appellate tender evaluation committees", "Government tenders must be awarded to the highest bidder", "No government contracts can be reviewed by High Courts"], "Courts intervene in tenders only on grounds of arbitrariness, bias, mala fides, or procedural irrationality."),
    ("The legal doctrine of 'Res Judicata' (Section 11 CPC) applies to writ petitions under Article 32/226 EXCEPT in:", "Petitions for the writ of Habeas Corpus involving personal liberty", ["Petitions for Mandamus on service promotions", "Petitions for Certiorari challenging tax assessments", "Petitions for Quo-Warranto regarding municipal offices"], "Ghulam Sarwar v. Union of India established that consecutive habeas corpus petitions lie on fresh grounds of liberty."),
    ("In administrative inquiries, what is the 'Preponderance of Probabilities' standard of proof compared to criminal trials?", "Disciplinary inquiries require proof on balance of probabilities, not proof beyond reasonable doubt", ["Disciplinary inquiries require 100% mathematical certainty", "Disciplinary inquiries use identical criminal court standards", "No evidence of any kind is required in administrative hearings"], "An employee can be dismissed on departmental inquiry even if acquitted in criminal court on benefit of doubt."),
    ("The legal effect of a successful challenge on grounds of 'Procedural Ultra Vires' is that:", "The subordinate rule or administrative order is quashed for failure to follow mandatory statutory procedure", ["The parent Act of Parliament is declared permanently unconstitutional", "The minister is automatically suspended from the legislature", "The entire department is placed under military administration"], "Non-compliance with mandatory procedural requirements (e.g., prior publication, mandatory consultation) voids the rule."),
    ("Under the Central Administrative Tribunal rules, an application challenging a service grievance must be filed within:", "One year from the date on which final rejection order was passed", ["Three years from the date of initial joining", "Thirty days without any condonation of delay", "Six months from the date of retirement"], "Section 21 of the Administrative Tribunals Act 1985 prescribes a one-year limitation period, extendable on sufficient cause."),
    ("In administrative law, the 'Doctrine of Unreasonableness' formulated in Padfield v. Minister of Agriculture (1968) established:", "Ministers cannot exercise statutory discretion to frustrate the underlying policy and objects of the parent statute", ["Ministers possess unfettered discretion to refuse inquiries without reason", "Courts must accept all ministerial policy affidavits as unchallengeable", "Parliament cannot enact regulatory statutes"], "Discretion given to a minister is not absolute; it must be exercised to promote the statutory policy.")
]

admin_law_hard = [
    ("In the landmark case of S.P. Sampath Kumar v. Union of India (1987), the Supreme Court held that Administrative Tribunals:", "Can serve as effective institutional substitutes for High Courts IF they possess equivalent independence and stature", ["Are inherently unconstitutional under all circumstances", "Can operate without any judicial members", "Are superior in rank to the Supreme Court of India"], "Justice Bhagwati held tribunals must be real judicial substitutes with independent selection benches."),
    ("In Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992), the Constitution Bench examined the Tenth Schedule (Anti-Defection) and held that:", "The Speaker's decision is that of a tribunal subject to judicial review on grounds of mala fides, perversity, or natural justice", ["The Speaker's decision enjoys absolute, unchallengeable judicial immunity", "Decisions of the Speaker cannot be challenged in any court ever", "The Tenth Schedule is completely void for violating basic structure"], "The Court held paragraph 6(1) ouster clause invalid, subjecting Speakers' decisions to post-decisional judicial review."),
    ("The foundational House of Lords ruling in Padfield v. Minister of Agriculture, Fisheries and Food (1968) established that:", "Executive discretion is never unfettered; refusal to refer a complaint based on bad or undisclosed reasons is ultra vires", ["Ministers have unreviewable personal prerogative to bury complaints", "Parliamentary statutes cannot confer discretion on ministers", "Courts cannot issue mandamus against a cabinet minister"], "Lord Reid ruled that an executive minister cannot exercise discretion to thwart the statutory purpose of the Act."),
    ("The landmark US Supreme Court doctrine of 'Chevron Deference' (Chevron U.S.A. v. NRDC, 1984) held that courts must:", "Defer to an agency's reasonable interpretation of an ambiguous statutory provision it administers", ["Strike down all regulations issued by federal agencies immediately", "Re-interpret all statutory provisions de novo without agency input", "Delegate all regulatory authority to state governors"], "Chevron established a two-step framework deferring to agency expertise when legislative intent is ambiguous."),
    ("The US Supreme Court's 'Major Questions Doctrine' (West Virginia v. EPA, 2022) counterbalanced Chevron by holding that:", "Agencies asserting broad authority on issues of vast economic and political significance require clear congressional authorization", ["Agencies have infinite plenary authority over all national industries", "Congress cannot enact any environmental regulatory statutes", "Presidents can unilaterally suspend federal corporate taxes"], "Major policy decisions cannot be inferred from vague statutory delegations without explicit legislative mandate."),
    ("In administrative law, the 'Doctrine of Substantive Legitimate Expectation' differs from procedural expectation by:", "Protecting the actual substantive benefit promised, unless overriding public interest justifies departure", ["Requiring only that an applicant be granted a polite five-minute interview", "Applying only to private commercial contracts between corporations", "Being completely unenforceable in any constitutional court"], "Courts will hold public authorities to substantive representations where denial would amount to abuse of power."),
    ("In R (Daly) v. Secretary of State for the Home Department (2001), Lord Steyn held that the 'Proportionality Test':", "Involves deeper, more rigorous scrutiny than Wednesbury review, evaluating intensity and necessity of rights infringement", ["Is identical in every respect to the classic Wednesbury irrationality test", "Cannot be applied to cases involving fundamental human rights", "Requires courts to defer unconditionally to prison authorities"], "Proportionality examines whether the objective is important enough, rationally connected, and the least restrictive means."),
    ("The Supreme Court in Ramana Dayaram Shetty v. International Airport Authority of India (1979) established that:", "The government must adhere to standards it has set; arbitrary departure from tender eligibility violates Article 14", ["The state can award public contracts to anyone it pleases without rules", "Public airport tenders are exempt from the Constitution", "Unsuccessful bidders have no legal right to challenge corrupt tenders"], "Justice Bhagwati held the executive is bound by its own eligibility conditions and cannot act arbitrarily."),
    ("In Shrilekha Vidyarthi v. State of UP (1991), the Supreme Court quashed en masse termination of government counsel, ruling:", "State action in all spheres, including contractual and appointment matters, must be non-arbitrary under Article 14", ["Government can terminate public prosecutors arbitrarily at will without reason", "Government advocates are exempt from constitutional protections", "Article 14 applies only to statutory civil servants, not professionals"], "The Court held that the state's contractual actions are not immune from the requirement of basic fairness."),
    ("In Express Newspapers v. Union of India (1986), the Supreme Court struck down eviction notices on grounds of:", "'Malice in Law' (acting with oblique motive to stifle freedom of the press)", ["Pecuniary financial corruption by municipal staff", "Lack of physical ink signatures on the notices", "The company being foreign-owned"], "Malice in law occurs when an administrative power is used to achieve an ulterior, legally unauthorized purpose."),
    ("The concept of 'Institutional Bias' (Official Bias) in administrative law was analyzed in Gullapalli Nageswara Rao (G.N. Rao) case where:", "The Secretary of the Transport Department heard objections against the nationalization scheme proposed by his own department", ["The Minister accepted personal financial bribes on live television", "The Judge owned shares in the private bus company", "The Governor issued an emergency curfew"], "The Court held the Secretary was the author of the scheme and could not impartially decide citizen objections against it."),
    ("In the Second G.N. Rao case (1959), the Supreme Court invalidated a nationalization order because:", "One official heard the oral objections, but an entirely different minister decided the case ('One who hears must decide')", ["No written notices were printed in newspapers", "The transport vehicles were manufactured abroad", "The private bus operators refused to attend"], "A fundamental rule of natural justice is that the authority that hears must decide; proxy decisions are void."),
    ("In Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978), the Supreme Court held that the procedure established by law under Article 21 must be:", "'Right, just, and fair, and not arbitrary, fanciful, or oppressive'", ["Any formal procedure enacted by Parliament regardless of harshness", "Determined exclusively by executive police manual guidelines", "Applicable only to Indian territory, not foreign passports"], "Article 21 was organically linked with Article 14 and Article 19, reading substantive natural justice into procedural law."),
    ("The legal doctrine of 'De Minimis Non Curat Lex' applied in administrative review implies:", "The law does not concern itself with trifles or negligible, inconsequential technical infractions", ["Courts must punish every microscopic grammatical error in filings", "Minor delays in filing appeals cannot be condoned under any circumstance", "All administrative orders are exempt from review"], "Courts will not quash administrative proceedings over negligible technical irregularities causing zero actual prejudice."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Prejudice' formulated in State Bank of Patiala v. S.K. Sharma (1996) regarding natural justice holds that:", "Violations of non-mandatory procedural rules invalidate orders only if the employee proves substantive prejudice", ["Any procedural error automatically voids an order even without prejudice", "Natural justice has no application in bank employee inquiries", "Employers never need to follow any disciplinary procedures"], "The Court distinguished between core natural justice (facets of no notice) and auxiliary procedural technicalities."),
    ("In Managing Director, ECIL v. B. Karunakar (1993), the Constitution Bench resolved the inquiry report dilemma by ruling:", "The delinquent employee must be supplied a copy of the inquiry report before the disciplinary authority decides on penalty", ["The inquiry report must be kept permanently secret from the employee", "Supplying the inquiry report voids the entire proceeding automatically", "Only criminal courts can view departmental inquiry reports"], "Supplying the inquiry report enables the employee to point out errors and defend themselves before final punishment."),
    ("In Rohtas Industries v. S.D. Agarwal (1969), the Supreme Court reviewed the subjective satisfaction of the executive, holding that:", "Courts can review whether the factual material upon which satisfaction is based is relevant and rational", ["Executive subjective satisfaction is completely unchallengeable in court", "Companies have no legal standing to challenge government inspectors", "Subjective satisfaction requires no factual material of any kind"], "If the grounds relied upon are irrelevant or extraneous, the resulting satisfaction and order are ultra vires."),
    ("The 'Collateral Attack' (Indirect Challenge) in administrative law refers to challenging an administrative order:", "In subsequent enforcement proceedings or civil litigation, rather than through direct judicial review", ["By filing physical protests on municipal public highways", "Through confidential diplomatic back-channel letters", "By appealing to the International Court of Justice"], "An invalid void order can be attacked collaterally when authorities attempt to enforce it against an individual."),
    ("In Barium Chemicals Ltd v. Company Law Board (1967), Justice Hidayatullah and Justice Shelat established that:", "Words like 'if in the opinion of the Central Government' do not confer unreviewable subjective power", ["Government opinion is an inviolable absolute royal prerogative", "Company Law Board can initiate investigations without any reason", "Private shareholders cannot challenge administrative inspectors"], "The formation of administrative opinion is subject to review if based on non-existent or irrelevant grounds."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Incuria' (Per Incuriam) in administrative and judicial precedent denotes a decision rendered:", "In ignorance of or through oversight of an applicable binding statutory provision or higher court precedent", ["With criminal fraudulent intent by corrupt judges", "During an emergency curfew after midnight", "By an unconfirmed temporary probationary magistrate"], "A per incuriam decision lacks binding precedential authority under the doctrine of stare decisis."),
    ("In Swadeshi Cotton Mills v. Union of India (1981), the Supreme Court ruled on post-decisional hearing, holding that:", "Even where urgent statutory takeover excludes prior hearing, natural justice requires a prompt post-decisional hearing", ["Urgent economic takeovers completely eliminate natural justice forever", "Government can seize private mills without ever hearing the owners", "Cotton mills are exempt from all constitutional fundamental rights"], "Natural justice is not a rigid straitjacket; it flexes to accommodate urgency through post-decisional hearings."),
    ("In Mohinder Singh Gill v. Chief Election Commissioner (1977), Justice Krishna Iyer laid down the cardinal administrative rule that:", "An administrative order must be judged solely on the reasons recorded in the order itself, not reasons added later by affidavit", ["Authorities can invent new reasons during court hearings through affidavits", "The Election Commission is completely exempt from judicial review", "Oral election decisions require no contemporaneous records"], "Public orders made by authorities cannot be supplemented by fresh reasons in the shape of affidavits during litigation."),
    ("The doctrine of 'Jurisdictional Error' after the landmark ruling in Anisminic Ltd (1969) encompasses:", "Misconstruing the statute, considering irrelevant matters, violating natural justice, or exceeding statutory boundaries", ["Only errors regarding the geographical boundary of a court room", "Typographical spelling mistakes made by administrative stenographers", "Disagreeing with the personal political views of the minister"], "Any material error of law that causes a tribunal to ask the wrong question goes to jurisdiction and voids the order."),
    ("The constitutional significance of Article 323B compared to Article 323A is that:", "Article 323A applies exclusively to public service matters; Article 323B covers diverse subjects like taxation, land, and rent", ["Article 323B applies only to the armed forces", "Article 323A can be enacted by State Assemblies", "There is no functional difference between them"], "Article 323A allows Parliament to create civil service tribunals; 323B allows Union and States to create tribunals for other fields."),
    ("In Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (Second Judges Case, 1993), the Court ruled on judicial appointments:", "'Consultation' under Article 124 means 'Concurrence' of the Chief Justice of India and the Collegium", ["The President has absolute unconstrained power to appoint any lawyer as judge", "The Union Law Minister possesses sole veto power over judicial candidates", "Judges must be elected by nationwide popular referendum"], "The Collegium system of appointment was instituted to safeguard judicial independence from executive dominance."),
    ("In National Judicial Appointments Commission (NJAC) Case (Fourth Judges Case, 2015), the Supreme Court struck down the 99th Amendment because:", "Inclusion of the Union Law Minister and two eminent persons infringed judicial independence, violating Basic Structure", ["Parliament lacked constitutional legislative competence to amend Article 124", "The amendment was passed without a two-thirds majority in Rajya Sabha", "State legislatures refused to ratify the amendment"], "The Constitution Bench held that judicial appointments require primacy of judiciary to ensure independence."),
    ("The concept of 'Curative Petition' recognized in Rupa Ashok Hurra v. Ashok Hurra (2002) requires certifying that:", "There was a violation of natural justice (audi alteram partem) or demonstrable bias by a judge", ["The petitioner has exhausted all personal monetary funds", "The case involves a commercial trade contract over Rs 100 crore", "The original trial was conducted in an English court"], "Senior advocates must certify that genuine grounds of bias or natural justice denial warrant curative review."),
    ("In State of Punjab v. V.K. Khanna (2001), the Supreme Court quashed a charge-sheet against a senior civil servant on grounds of:", "Malice in fact and bias evidenced by hasty issuance following political regime change", ["Lack of jurisdiction of the state government to issue charge-sheets", "The civil servant having diplomatic foreign immunity", "The charge-sheet being written in the Punjabi language"], "The court held that issuance of a charge-sheet with predetermined guilt without preliminary inquiry demonstrates actual bias."),
    ("In administrative tort liability, the 'Non-Sovereign Function' exception established in Vidyawati case (1962) held that:", "State is vicariously liable in tort for rash driving by government drivers just like an ordinary employer", ["The state can never be held liable for vehicle accidents under any circumstance", "Government drivers possess absolute sovereign immunity", "Tort claims against the state can be filed only in international courts"], "Chief Justice B.P. Sinha held that republican India cannot claim the feudal sovereign immunity of the British Crown."),
    ("The 'Doctrine of Public Interest Immunity' (Crown Privilege) under Section 123 of Indian Evidence Act enables the state to:", "Withhold disclosure of unpublished official records relating to affairs of state if disclosure injures public interest", ["Confiscate private assets without payment of compensation", "Imprison citizens without filing criminal charges", "Exempt all cabinet ministers from income tax filing"], "The court balances injury to public interest against the injury to administration of justice (S.P. Gupta case)."),
    ("In administrative law, the 'Test of Real Danger of Bias' (R v. Gough) was superseded in British and Commonwealth jurisprudence by:", "The 'Fair-Minded and Informed Observer' test (Porter v. Magill, 2001)", ["The Subjective Certainty of the Trial Magistrate test", "The Absolute Mathematical Impossibility test", "The Sovereign Diplomatic Prerogative test"], "The test asks whether a fair-minded and informed observer, having considered the facts, would conclude there was a real possibility of bias."),
    ("The legal effect of the 'Doctrine of Transferred Malice' or 'Colourable Legislation' in administrative law is:", "You cannot do indirectly that which the law prohibits you from doing directly ('Quando aliquid prohibetur ex directo...')", ["Administrative agencies can transfer criminal convictions between family members", "Subordinate legislation can be drafted anonymously by foreign lawyers", "Color coding of administrative files determines legal validity"], "Colourable exercise of power occurs when an authority cloaks an unauthorized act under the guise of an ostensible lawful power."),
    ("In administrative law, 'Judicial Self-Restraint' in review of economic and technical policy (e.g., Telecom, Electricity Tariffs) dictates that:", "Courts refrain from substituting their own economic opinions for specialized regulatory expert bodies unless arbitrary", ["Courts must re-calculate all commercial tariffs de novo in every case", "Regulatory agencies are banned from hiring economists", "Economic policies cannot be challenged under any constitutional provision"], "Courts acknowledge their lack of specialized technical expertise in complex economic regulation, limiting review to legality.")
]
